(1) Where the Court is required to determine whether to grant bail in respect of an offence committed under this Act, the Court shall consider-

(a)	the need to protect the applicant from violence;
(b)	the welfare of a child or dependant of whom the respondent or the applicant has custody or care of that child or dependant;
(c)	the welfare of any child or dependant being a member of the household; and
(d)	any hardship that may be caused to the respondent or other members of the family if bail is not granted.
(2)	The Court in granting bail, may order that the recognisance be subject to any one or more of the following conditions, as it considers appropriate-
(a)	that the respondent not harass or molest or procure another person to harass or molest the applicant;
(b)	that the respondent not be present on the premises on which the applicant works or resides;
(c)	that the respondent not be in a locality in which is situated the premises in which the applicant works or resides; and

(d)	that the respondent not be in a locality or on the premises in which is situated the place of education of a child, if it is necessary to protect the welfare of that child.
(3)	Where a police officer believes on reasonable grounds that a person, who has been admitted to bail subject to one or more conditions, has failed to comply with a condition of the recognisance, the police officer may arrest the person without a warrant.

